      "rev_n_1_a8a18b_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_a8a18b_0",
        "node_id": "n_1_a8a18b",
        "thought_text": "Sort the difficulty array and identify the split point where exactly N/2 problems are smaller than K and N/2 are greater than or equal to K. The valid K values will be integers strictly between the largest of the lower half and the smallest of the upper half.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794505469.2363033
      },
      "rev_n_2_c6a557_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_c6a557_0",
        "node_id": "n_2_c6a557",
        "thought_text": "Iterate through all possible integer values of K from 1 to 100,000, count the number of problems for ARCs and ABCs for each K, and increment a counter if the counts are equal. This brute-force approach is feasible given the constraints.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794505469.2363167
      },
      "rev_n_3_3d2b8c_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_3d2b8c_0",
        "node_id": "n_3_3d2b8c",
        "thought_text": "Use a frequency array or hash map to count occurrences of each difficulty. Then, simulate increasing K from 1 upwards, maintaining a running count of problems assigned to ABCs and ARCs, stopping when the split condition is met or K exceeds the maximum difficulty.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794505469.2363253
      },
      "rev_n_4_b4a836_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_b4a836_0",
        "node_id": "n_4_b4a836",
        "thought_text": "Sort the difficulties and find the gap between the N/2-th smallest and the (N/2+1)-th smallest element; the count of valid K is the difference between these two values.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-2006332671008782042",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794505498.0715358
      },
      "rev_n_5_042406_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_5_042406_0",
        "node_id": "n_5_042406",
        "thought_text": "Sort the array, then iterate through possible integer values between the median lower bound and median upper bound to count how many satisfy the condition directly.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-2006332671008782042",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794505498.0715516
      },
      "rev_n_6_9875b1_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_6_9875b1_0",
        "node_id": "n_6_9875b1",
        "thought_text": "Use a frequency array or hash map to count occurrences of each difficulty, then simulate the split point by accumulating counts until reaching N/2 to determine the valid range for K.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-2006332671008782042",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794505498.07156
      },
      "rev_n_7_9e3c7b_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_7_9e3c7b_0",
        "node_id": "n_7_9e3c7b",
        "thought_text": "Sort the difficulty array and identify the split point where exactly N/2 problems are smaller than K and N/2 are greater than or equal to K. The valid K values will form a contiguous range between the (N/2)-th smallest and (N/2+1)-th smallest difficulty values.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "4688140274975450709",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794505508.1264255
      },
      "rev_n_8_49396d_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_8_49396d_0",
        "node_id": "n_8_49396d",
        "thought_text": "Use a frequency array or hash map to count occurrences of each difficulty. Iterate through sorted unique difficulty values to find the range of K where the cumulative count of problems with difficulty < K equals N/2.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "4688140274975450709",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794505508.1264489
      },
      "rev_n_9_867474_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_9_867474_0",
        "node_id": "n_9_867474",
        "thought_text": "Since N is up to 10^5, simulate the process by sorting the array once in O(N log N) time, then calculate the answer in O(1) by checking the values at indices N/2 - 1 and N/2 in the sorted list.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "4688140274975450709",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794505508.126462
      },
      "rev_n_10_70dd9d_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_10_70dd9d_0",
        "node_id": "n_10_70dd9d",
        "thought_text": "Sort the difficulties and find the median. Since N is even, any K strictly between the N/2-th and (N/2 + 1)-th smallest values will split the array into two equal halves. The number of such integers is the difference between these two values.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-1214943786743246878",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794505521.5296285
      },
      "rev_n_11_3b3617_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_11_3b3617_0",
        "node_id": "n_11_3b3617",
        "thought_text": "Use a frequency map to count occurrences of each difficulty level. Iterate through possible K values from 1 to max(d)+1, maintaining a running count of problems assigned to ABCs (d < K) and ARCs (d >= K). Count how many K values result in both counts being N/2.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-1214943786743246878",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794505521.5296464
      },
      "rev_n_12_994e3f_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_12_994e3f_0",
        "node_id": "n_12_994e3f",
        "thought_text": "Sort the array and check the gap between the element at index N/2 - 1 and index N/2 (0-indexed). The valid K values are all integers strictly greater than the smaller element and strictly less than or equal to the larger element, but since the condition is d < K for ABCs and d >= K for ARCs, the valid range is (d[N/2-1], d[N/2]]. The count is d[N/2] - d[N/2-1].",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-1214943786743246878",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794505521.529654
      },
      "rev_n_13_ae561b_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_13_ae561b_0",
        "node_id": "n_13_ae561b",
        "thought_text": "Sort the array d and identify the split point at index N/2. The valid K values are integers strictly greater than d[N/2 - 1] (the largest of the lower half) and strictly less than or equal to d[N/2] (the smallest of the upper half). The count is d[N/2] - d[N/2 - 1].",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "2729823394273829266",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794505568.5456793
      },
      "rev_n_14_616233_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_14_616233_0",
        "node_id": "n_14_616233",
        "thought_text": "Use a frequency array or hash map to count occurrences of each difficulty. Iterate through possible K values from 1 to 100001, maintaining a running count of problems with difficulty >= K and < K. Stop when the split condition is met or impossible.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "2729823394273829266",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794505568.545697
      },
      "rev_n_15_f3a00e_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_15_f3a00e_0",
        "node_id": "n_15_f3a00e",
        "thought_text": "Since N is even, the median lies between the two middle elements. The number of valid K is simply the difference between the element at index N/2 and the element at index N/2 - 1 in the sorted list, assuming 0-based indexing.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "2729823394273829266",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794505568.5457063
      },
      "rev_n_16_9bb28e_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_16_9bb28e_0",
        "node_id": "n_16_9bb28e",
        "thought_text": "Sort the difficulties and find the split point between the lower N/2 and upper N/2 elements. The valid K values are integers strictly greater than the maximum of the lower half and strictly less than the minimum of the upper half. The count is simply the difference between these two medians.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-158508391114283692",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794505600.807165
      },
      "rev_n_17_eef440_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_17_eef440_0",
        "node_id": "n_17_eef440",
        "thought_text": "After sorting, the number of valid K values is determined by the gap between the (N/2)-th element and the (N/2 + 1)-th element. If the gap is 0, the answer is 0; otherwise, it is the gap size. This avoids iterating through all possible K values.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-158508391114283692",
